WEBVTT

00:00:00.139 --> 00:00:02.731
{happy}Hello, and welcome to Shah Spectrum.

00:00:02.731 --> 00:00:04.859
If you're new here, welcome.

00:00:04.859 --> 00:00:09.049
If you're back again, it's lovely to have you with us once more.

00:00:09.049 --> 00:00:15.048
{happy}What happens to a company when one very big product lands in the middle of

00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:15.648
its year?

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:21.872
One of the most talked-about games ever has just gone on sale, and the company

00:00:21.872 --> 00:00:24.619
behind it has just given us its numbers.

00:00:24.619 --> 00:00:25.370
{happy}I'm Mittal Shah.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:28.336
Let's look at the numbers behind a video game.

00:00:28.336 --> 00:00:30.327
{fluent}First, what happened.

00:00:30.327 --> 00:00:34.590
Grand Theft Auto VI, the new game from Rockstar Games, went on sale on the

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:38.719
nineteenth of November, on PlayStation 5 and Xbox Series X and S.

00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:45.103
Rockstar is owned by Take-Two Interactive, an American company listed on the Nasdaq

00:00:45.103 --> 00:00:45.780
exchange.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.261
A standard copy lists at eighty dollars.

00:00:48.682 --> 00:00:53.562
In its latest release, Take-Two gave no launch sales figure.

00:00:53.562 --> 00:00:58.797
An analytics firm called Sensor Tower, which tracks digital sales on the console

00:00:58.797 --> 00:01:04.032
stores, estimated about nineteen million copies and about one point eight billion

00:01:04.032 --> 00:01:05.542
dollars by the morning of launch day.

00:01:05.542 --> 00:01:09.360
That is an estimate, and it covers digital sales only.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:15.007
Ten days before launch, Take-Two's chief executive said pre-orders were "continuing

00:01:15.007 --> 00:01:20.173
to reach unprecedented levels", but gave no figure.

00:01:20.621 --> 00:01:23.956
Now, why does one game matter so much to one company?

00:01:23.956 --> 00:01:27.386
Look at what Take-Two reported on the ninth of November.

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:31.738
In the three months to September, its net bookings were one point six billion

00:01:31.738 --> 00:01:34.679
dollars, down eighteen per cent from a year earlier.

00:01:34.679 --> 00:01:40.491
Net bookings is the company's own measure of what it sells in a period, digital and

00:01:40.491 --> 00:01:43.433
physical, including in-game purchases.

00:01:43.433 --> 00:01:45.178
It is sales, not profit.

00:01:45.178 --> 00:01:48.919
So the quarter just before the launch was a weaker one.

00:01:49.317 --> 00:01:52.671
Yet the company kept its outlook for the full year to March:

00:01:52.671 --> 00:01:55.398
net bookings of eight to eight point two billion dollars.

00:01:55.398 --> 00:02:00.634
And for the October to December quarter alone, it expects three point three five to

00:02:00.634 --> 00:02:01.927
three point five billion.

00:02:01.927 --> 00:02:04.944
That is more than double the quarter before.

00:02:04.944 --> 00:02:09.958
For scale, the full-year outlook is eight billion dollars or more, so that one

00:02:09.958 --> 00:02:13.440
quarter alone is roughly two-fifths of it.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.776
The release does not say how much of it comes from the new game, but the timing

00:02:17.776 --> 00:02:19.645
coincides with the launch.

00:02:20.062 --> 00:02:22.560
On the same thread, how did the company get through the wait?

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:27.403
Of that one point six billion dollars, eighty-one per cent was what Take-Two calls

00:02:27.403 --> 00:02:30.869
recurrent consumer spending.

00:02:30.869 --> 00:02:36.027
That is money from ongoing play in games already out, such as virtual currency and

00:02:36.027 --> 00:02:36.912
add-on content.

00:02:36.912 --> 00:02:38.666
So money from games already out was paying the bills.

00:02:38.666 --> 00:02:44.167
Grand Theft Auto V, which came out back in 2013, has sold more than two hundred

00:02:44.167 --> 00:02:44.925
million copies.

00:02:45.258 --> 00:02:47.399
Next, a caution about comparing numbers.

00:02:47.399 --> 00:02:53.168
The analytics firm estimates what shoppers spend in the digital stores, and only

00:02:53.168 --> 00:02:53.768
digital.

00:02:53.726 --> 00:02:56.012
Take-Two counts net bookings, its own measure.

00:02:56.012 --> 00:02:57.764
They are not the same thing.

00:02:57.764 --> 00:03:04.322
So the estimate of about one point eight billion dollars cannot be read as the

00:03:04.322 --> 00:03:05.634
company's launch figure.

00:03:05.634 --> 00:03:07.695
It only gives a sense of scale.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:09.740
Finally, what do we not know?

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.608
We do not have Take-Two's own count of copies sold.

00:03:12.608 --> 00:03:17.809
We do not know how much of the quarter's outlook the launch will deliver, or how

00:03:17.809 --> 00:03:20.626
much players keep spending after the first few weeks.

00:03:20.626 --> 00:03:25.123
The company's own figures will arrive when the company next reports its results.

00:03:25.123 --> 00:03:26.244
{happy}To sum up:

00:03:26.244 --> 00:03:32.325
a quarter with sales down eighteen per cent, a year outlook that includes a December

00:03:32.325 --> 00:03:36.674
quarter more than twice as big, and money from older games paying the bills while

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:38.323
the new one lands.

00:03:38.323 --> 00:03:42.882
The company's outlook points to a much bigger quarter ahead, and the timing

00:03:42.882 --> 00:03:44.582
coincides with the launch!

00:03:44.582 --> 00:03:48.434
{happy}This is news and explanation, not investment advice.

00:03:48.434 --> 00:03:51.682
Thanks for listening, and see you in the next episode!

